{"name":"Mt. Hood White","hex":"#e7e9e6","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"mt.","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["Abbreviation of mount.","Abbreviation of metre."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/mt."},{"term":"hood","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A covering for the head, usually attached to a larger garment such as a jacket or cloak.\n A head covering placed on falcons to inhibit their vision.\n A head and neck covering placed on horses to protect against insects and sunlight, to slow coat growth and for warmth.","A head covering placed on falcons to inhibit their vision."]},{"part":"Verb","definitions":["To cover (something) with a hood.","To extend out from (something), in the manner of a hood."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/hood"},{"term":"white","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Bright and colourless; reflecting equal quantities of all frequencies of visible light.","Of or relating to Europeans or those of European descent, regardless if their skin has cool or warm undertones."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The color of snow or milk; the color of light containing equal amounts of all visible wavelengths.","A person of European descent with light-colored skin."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/white"}],"entries":[{"title":"Mount Hood","blocks":[{"text":"Mount Hood is an active stratovolcano in the Cascade Range and is a member of the Cascade Volcanic Arc. It was formed by a subduction zone on the Pacific Coast and rests in the Pacific Northwest region of the United States. It is located about 50 miles (80 km) east-east-southeast of Portland, on the border between Clackamas and Hood River counties, and forms part of the Mount Hood National Forest. With a summit elevation of 11,249 feet (3,429 m), it is the highest mountain in the U.S. state of Oregon and is the fourth highest in the Cascade Range."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/1/1c/Oregon_Mount_Hood_from_Trillium_Lake_2024.jpg/330px-Oregon_Mount_Hood_from_Trillium_Lake_2024.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Mount_Hood","wikidata":"Q275662"},{"title":"White","blocks":[{"text":"White is the lightest color and is achromatic. It is the color of objects such as snow, chalk, and milk, and is the opposite of black. White objects fully reflect and scatter all the visible wavelengths of light. White on television and computer screens is created by a mixture of red, blue, and green light. The color white can be given with white pigments, especially titanium dioxide."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/e/e2/Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg/330px-Delphinapterus_leucas_2.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/White","wikidata":"Q23444"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Mount_Hood%2C_Oregon_by_William_Keith%2C_c1881-83.jpg/500px-Mount_Hood%2C_Oregon_by_William_Keith%2C_c1881-83.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/7/7f/Mount_Hood%2C_Oregon_by_William_Keith%2C_c1881-83.jpg/500px-Mount_Hood%2C_Oregon_by_William_Keith%2C_c1881-83.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"William Keith (American, 1838–1911). Mount Hood, Oregon, c. 1881–1883. Brooklyn Museum.","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AMount_Hood%2C_Oregon_by_William_Keith%2C_c1881-83.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Mount_Hood_2619s.jpg/500px-Mount_Hood_2619s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/6/62/Mount_Hood_2619s.jpg/1280px-Mount_Hood_2619s.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaig